A||||4||GT Bastard||Prepping Fiero body panels.... ???????||||3||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||04-08-2000||01:25 PM||||I'm a little ignorant when it comes to body work on these cars.  I've sanded a couple Camaro's, but would I need to use some sort of stripper on my Fiero's fender?  I was planning on using a flexible primer, but would it be ok to just sand the fender?  Also, I've heard there really isn't a difference between the primer in spray-cans and the professional stuff.  Is this correct?<BR>thanks!!!!!!||||reg||3
Z||000001||Cooter||04-08-2000||04:48 PM||||I really dont like strippers because sometimes the residue they leave behind makes it hard for paint to stick. There are strippers for urethane bumpers, so it should work on the Fiero panels but try it somewhere safe first. Unless the old paint is really cracked or coming off, there is no real need to strip it down to the bare plastic.<P>About the primers, I have never seen a primer from a spray can that was any good. Every time I work on something that has had spray can primer on it, the top coat sealer or base-coat causes it to blister.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||04-08-2000||08:36 PM||||I recently inquired about repainting the Grape Ape and asked about the flex agent.  The body shop (very reputable) said that most, if not all, of the new paint doesn't need any.  10 years ago it would have, though.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula||04-08-2000||08:50 PM||||what would a fiero look like completly stripped down to the plastic and polished?<P>would it be black or yellow?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Gokart||04-08-2000||09:21 PM||||I was told the same thing about today's paints and it's epoxy additive that does not require any of the old flexible chemical. I've had great success with Dupli-color red/oxide primer in a can. The nozzle duplicates the one spray guns have instead of "hair spray" nozzles. It sands nice and even, shows any defects and adheres to everything I've put on after.<p>[This message has been edited by Gokart (edited 04-08-2000).]||||reg||14
